      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14208916/assets/e714ae145a8d9f45dd2139f28866e6919fcf505d547352f0e615947583360723.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ is given above. The points $A,B,C,$ and $D$ lie on the graph, as shown. What is the value of $f^{-1}(-3)?$",
      "worked_solution": "The point $D$ on the graph has coordinates $(2,-3).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nf(2) = -3.\n$$\n\nApplying $f^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that $f^{-1}\\left(f(x)\\right) = x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(f(2)) & ={f}^{-1}(-3) \\\\ 2 & ={f}^{-1}(-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f^{-1}(-3) = 2.$",
